  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showery and breezy today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSurface/upper lows vertically stack across the Maritimes today \nslowing the movement of the system. A disturbance moving around\nthe Maritimes system will rotate across the region today. \nIsolated/scattered showers will persist across the region \ntoday, then diminish tonight. The Maritimes system slowly moves\neast Sunday. Dependent on how rapidly the Maritimes system \nmoves, isolated/scattered showers wrapping around the system\ncould persist across eastern portions of the forecast area \nearly Sunday. The better chances would occur along the \nMaine/New Brunswick border. The pressure gradient between the \nMaritimes low and high pressure to the west will help support \nbreezy conditions today and Sunday. North/northeast winds will \ngust up to 20 to 25 mph today and Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances remain limited through midweek,\naccompanied by near to below normal temperatures. The next best\nchance for precipitation arrives Wednesday night into Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe low pressure system shifts to the NE and exits over the\nMaritimes by Monday. A large upper level ridge will progress \nacross the region. Surface high pressure should keep clouds away\nSunday night and Monday. This should result in temps in the low\n40s Sunday night and upper 70s on Monday. Shortwave energy \nshould cross the region on Tuesday bringing cloud cover, and a\nslight chance of isolated showers, that will reduce high temps \nand increase low temps. Dry conditions with below normal low \ntemps will continue to Wednesday night. Ensembles show \nincreasing confidence in a large closed low pressure system \nmoving across Canada that could bring some soaking rain and\ngusty winds. However, significant uncertainty on the track and \ntiming of the low exists across all deterministic models.",
